window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Everything the instructor explicitly said would be tested or heavily hinted. A ★ means it was said out loud."]},{"s":"Week 1▾","a":"week-1","k":["Week 1▾","Topic Name","★ A short, quick-reference bullet (about 20 words).","An unstarred bullet keeps its attribution: she hinted this would show up.","Not Tested","Record every \"you don't need to memorize this\" the moment it's said."]}]},{"f":"week1-pericardial-disease.html","t":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","secs":[{"s":"Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Myocarditis","The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid","Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade","Pulsus Paradoxus","Pericarditis on the ECG","Treating Pericarditis","Self-Test Flashcards","This is the first lecture of NUR 406, a short voiceover on pericardial disease. It covers myocarditis (inflammation of the heart muscle), then the pericardium itself: how inflammation of the pericardium leads to fluid buildup, pericardial e…"]},{"s":"Myocarditis▾","a":"myocarditis","k":["Myocarditis▾","Think back to normal A&P: the myocardium is the muscle layer of the heart, the muscle cells. Myocarditis is an inflammatory disease of the myocardium.","Range of severity — A wide variety of symptoms: it can be a pretty mild disorder with little to no discomfort, all the way up to heart failure and a lethal condition.","What happens to the cells — The inflammation comes with degeneration and necrosis of the cardiac myocytes. The inflammation causes direct injury to the myocytes, and over time it can create fibrosis and scarring that lead to structural chan…","Conduction — We often see a disruption in conduction through the heart. The tissue changes, inflammatory cytokines and T cell activation in the myocardium can result in abnormal electrical conduction.","Cause — Typically viruses. In the United States and Europe, viruses are the leading cause of myocarditis. The Coxsackie virus A and B are the majority of cases.","Treatment — Depends on how severe the symptoms are. Treated by giving antivirals; anti-inflammatories can also be used."]},{"s":"The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","a":"the-pericardium-pericardial-fluid","k":["The Pericardium & Pericardial Fluid▾","The pericardium is a sac-like membrane that surrounds the heart and the roots of the major blood vessels, and it helps the heart function properly. Between its inner and outer layers is the pericardial space.","Normal — A small amount, about 15 to 50 mL. — Acts like a lubricant between the inner and outer layers, for normal function of the heart.","Pericardial effusion — When the pericardium is inflamed, fluid accumulates in excess of what is normal. Effusion is that excess fluid accumulation. — Can compress the heart chambers and limit cardiac filling.","Cardiac tamponade — About 200 mL. — The fluid compresses the heart. The chambers are restricted and can't stretch and fill with blood as they normally would.","Cardiac tamponade is a medical emergency. The fluid has to be removed quickly, because the heart chambers can't stretch and fill with blood."]},{"s":"Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","a":"acute-pericarditis-vs-cardiac-tamponade","k":["Acute Pericarditis vs Cardiac Tamponade▾","The lecture describes what acute pericarditis looks like, then what changes once it progresses to cardiac tamponade and cardiac output falls.","Acute pericarditis","Chest pain: a sharp pain that worsens with deep breathing.","Fever, because of the inflammatory process.","Dyspnea (the lecture glosses it as \"some painful breathing\").","Pericardial friction rub on auscultation: a scratching sound, almost like rubbing on a balloon.","Muffled heart sounds, because you are listening to the heart pump through a layer of fluid.","ECG changes: elevations and also depressions.","Pulsus paradoxus is also named as a sign of acute pericarditis.","Cardiac tamponade","Signs of decreased cardiac output: hypotension, and the usual signs of a patient who is not being perfused appropriately, such as confusion and dizziness.","Jugular vein distension, from blood backing up in the system because of the decreased cardiac output.","Muffled heart sounds, from listening through a layer of fluid.","Pulsus paradoxus occurs in about 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade."]},{"s":"Pulsus Paradoxus▾","a":"pulsus-paradoxus","k":["Pulsus Paradoxus▾","What it is — A decrease in the systolic blood pressure when the patient breathes in (with inspiration).","Why it's a \"paradox\" — Typically your blood pressure should increase a little bit when you breathe in. Seeing a drop instead is the paradox.","What it indicates — The heart is not pumping properly, and something is impacting cardiac filling.","How you see it — Most clearly with an arterial line (an IV catheter in an artery), which gives real-time blood pressure: you can watch the pressure change as the patient breathes in.","How often — About 70 to 80 percent of patients experiencing cardiac tamponade.","The recording garbles the exact size of the systolic drop (\"a decrease in the systolic blood pressure of more and more\"), so no number is given here. If the slide shows a value, it can be added."]},{"s":"Pericarditis on the ECG▾","a":"pericarditis-on-the-ecg","k":["Pericarditis on the ECG▾","The ECG in pericarditis shows elements of ST depression as well as ST elevation. In some cases it looks similar to a patient having an MI, but the clinical symptoms will be different.","ST elevation in MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction. Compare the two if needed"]},{"s":"Treating Pericarditis▾","a":"treating-pericarditis","k":["Treating Pericarditis▾","NSAIDs (anti-inflammatories) — Anti-inflammatories are used. Aspirin may be seen occasionally.","Corticosteroids — \"Give or take\": sometimes they work really well, sometimes not so much.","Colchicine — Typically a gout medication, but recently found to work well for pericarditis that is not responding to NSAIDs.","Pericardiocentesis — For cardiac tamponade caused by the pericarditis. A needle is put directly into the pericardium and the fluid is drawn out. Because tamponade is a medical emergency, this happens quickly.","Aspirin for acute MI is a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acs-mi.html","t":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","secs":[{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","What ACS Is","The ACS Spectrum","Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina","What Makes Plaque Unstable","Why Plaque Ruptures","Unstable Angina","Stable Angina vs ACS","Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction","Acute Myocardial Infarction","Ischemia, Infarction & ATP","Zones of Damage","STEMI vs NSTEMI","The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the ACS voiceover: what ACS is, the spectrum from Prinzmetal's angina to STEMI, what makes plaque unstable and why it ruptures, unstable angina, how ACS differs from stable angina, the signs of MI, what happens to heart ti…"]},{"s":"What ACS Is▾","a":"what-acs-is","k":["What ACS Is▾","Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)","An umbrella term for situations in which the blood supply to the heart muscle is suddenly blocked or compromised.","It is certainly a medical emergency.","The blockage can come and go, or it can be sudden and complete. Either way, cardiac tissue is dying.","ACS covers three conditions: unstable angina, non-ST elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), and ST elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI).","The three have a similar pathophysiology, so the signs and symptoms can look similar. The ECG is what helps determine the next step: send the patient to the cath lab for reperfusion, or treat medically?","\"Time is tissue.\" The instructor says it several times: treat the blockage as quickly as possible to save as much tissue as possible."]},{"s":"The ACS Spectrum▾","a":"the-acs-spectrum","k":["The ACS Spectrum▾","The slide shows a progression from a normal vessel to complete occlusion. Stable angina has been covered before; ACS is the part of the picture where stable angina has become unstable.","Asymptomatic plaque — Some buildup of atherosclerotic plaque. — Nothing. They don't know it's there unless we go looking for it.","Stable angina — Atherosclerotic plaque buildup. — Signs and symptoms with exertion (strenuous activity).","Unstable angina — The clot is able to stay really small, and blood supply is still somewhat maintained. — Any new or changing chest pain.","NSTEMI — A larger buildup; blood flow is even more decreased. Some blood still gets through, but not enough. — A myocardial infarction without a change in the ST segment on the ECG.","STEMI — The vessel is completely and totally occluded by the clot. — A myocardial infarction with elevation of the ST segment on the ECG.","On the other side of the spectrum slide sits Prinzmetal's variant angina, which is a vasospasm of the coronary artery rather than plaque buildup (next section)."]},{"s":"Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","a":"prinzmetal-s-variant-or-vasospastic-angina","k":["Prinzmetal's (Variant or Vasospastic) Angina▾","Mechanism — The coronary artery vasospasms. It is not necessarily related to atherosclerotic plaque buildup; the spasm reduces oxygenated blood flow to the cardiac tissue. The spasms can increase a person's risk for myocardial ischemia.","Cause — A lot of times, damage to the endothelium of the coronary vessel. The spasms narrow the artery and decrease blood flow.","Plaque — These patients may or may not have atherosclerotic plaque.","When it happens — Oftentimes at rest: sleeping, sitting around, minimal activity. These are not the people who are overexerting themselves.","ECG — May show ST elevation. Monitor closely to see whether treatment is working and the ECG changes return to normal.","Treatment — Oftentimes nitrates, because they relax the spasm.","Outlook — Usually benign, but it can cause some serious dysrhythmias to be on the lookout for."]},{"s":"What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","a":"what-makes-plaque-unstable","k":["What Makes Plaque Unstable▾","Usually, when plaque becomes unstable, people develop ACS. These are people who already have atherosclerotic plaque in their coronary vessels.","Stable plaque","A pretty thick fibrous cap on top of the fat, which contributes to its stability.","Unstable plaque","The lipid core (the fat inside the vessel) gets bigger.","As the core grows, the fibrous cap gets thinner.","There is a component of inflammation; C-reactive protein (CRP) is a marker of inflammation in the body.","Smooth muscle cells proliferate into the intima (the lecture describes the intima as the middle layer of the blood vessel). These cells create the fibrous cap and bring in inflammatory components, increasing the risk of plaque rupture.","Assessing Unstable Plaque","A study that puts dye into the vessel shows how much narrowing is happening, so we can roughly estimate whether the lipid core is larger or smaller.","CRP levels show how much inflammation is going on."]},{"s":"Why Plaque Ruptures▾","a":"why-plaque-ruptures","k":["Why Plaque Ruptures▾","Rupture risk is higher with unstable plaque: a large lipid core, a thin fibrous cap, and ongoing inflammation. The slide walks through the theory step by step.","1. Trigger — Psychological stress, exercise, or circadian rhythms stimulate the sympathetic nervous system.","2. Sympathetic response — Epinephrine and norepinephrine are released: blood pressure and heart rate rise, and the heart works harder with stronger contractions.","3. Force on the vessels — Pushing out more blood with stronger contractions puts more force on the vessels, including the coronary vessels themselves.","4. Rupture — That force can lead to rupture of the plaque.","5. Platelets — Platelets come to the site and adhere to the ruptured plaque. They release substances that attract more platelets and can contribute to vasospasm.","6. Thrombus — A thrombus forms in that same area."]},{"s":"Unstable Angina▾","a":"unstable-angina","k":["Unstable Angina▾","What counts as unstable angina","Chest pain occurring for the first time. Consider it unstable immediately, until proven otherwise; it is a medical emergency because no pattern or history has been established.","In a patient who normally has stable angina, pain that is more severe than normal: for example, they have taken their first nitro and five minutes later the pain hasn't stopped or is getting worse.","Pain that is different: usually in the chest, but this time down one arm, down both arms, or radiating into the jaw.","It may happen at rest; the person may be awakened in the middle of the night.","What's happening in the vessel","The plaque ruptures and a thrombus forms. That rupture and clot formation is the real worry.","The size of the clot and of the blockage determine how much blood gets past to perfuse the tissue on the other side. A 30% occluded artery may still allow some flow around a clot; a 90% blockage leaves little room.","If there is no infarction, the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved, and some tissue was still perfused.","The ECG changes and then goes back to normal, and we typically don't see troponin changes.","Unstable angina is a medical emergency. Teach patients to recognize the signs, recognize it as an emergency, and call 911 right now. They should most definitely not drive themselves to the ER."]},{"s":"Stable Angina vs ACS▾","a":"stable-angina-vs-acs","k":["Stable Angina vs ACS▾","Teach patients the difference so they can identify it and seek care. The big difference is the pain: its severity and its duration.","Stable angina","Pain should last about 5 minutes, and be gone by maybe about 15 minutes.","May feel like a little bit of chest tightness.","Flares up with strenuous activity.","ACS","Chest pain lasts a lot longer.","No relief with nitroglycerin.","More severe: the sensation of an elephant sitting on your chest.","Other symptoms: diaphoresis, shortness of air, nausea and vomiting.","An impending sense of doom: they know something is not right but can't pinpoint it. Take them very, very seriously.","Signs in Men and Women","Research over roughly the last 50 years shows different signs and symptoms of ACS between men and women. These symptoms aren't limited to one or the other.","Typically associated with men","Chest pain.","Tingling or discomfort in the back, arms, neck or jaw.","Other symptoms (often reported by women)","Dizziness, nausea, vomiting, tiredness.","Heartburn: a lot of women with ACS say they took some Tums that morning because they thought it was heartburn.","Some of these can feel similar to stable angina. The particular concern is when the experience changes or gets worse."]},{"s":"Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"signs-symptoms-of-myocardial-infarction","k":["Signs & Symptoms of Myocardial Infarction▾","What the lecture lists","Diaphoresis and dyspnea.","Extreme anxiety; restless, can't sit still, moving around.","Impending sense of doom (\"I know something's wrong\") without being able to pinpoint it.","Levine sign: the fist to the chest, the \"Hollywood\" heart attack sign.","Pallor and weak pulses, from perfusion issues because the heart isn't working right.","Retrosternal (behind the sternum) crushing chest pain that can radiate to several parts of the body; the elephant on the chest."]},{"s":"Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","a":"acute-myocardial-infarction","k":["Acute Myocardial Infarction▾","What an acute MI is","Ruptured plaque plus thrombus that severely reduces or completely blocks blood flow to the heart muscle.","Think of ACS as a continuum; MI is at the end of that continuum.","It is ACS with prolonged ischemia that results in the death of myocardial tissue: an irreversible ischemic necrosis. These cells are never going to function properly again.","Infarction happens either because blood flow disruption goes on over a very long period of time, or because of a complete and total blockage.","How much tissue is lost depends on: where the infarction is (smaller or larger vessel, left or right side of the heart), how long blood supply has been affected, and how complete the blockage is. Partial flow over a few days can damage tiss…","MIs are classified by ECG findings: STEMI and NSTEMI.","ECG — Changes, then eventually goes back to normal. — Changes that stay; a repeat ECG won't go back to normal.","Troponin — Typically no change in cardiac enzymes. — Increases. Serial troponins trend up during the infarction, then trend down after the occlusion has been rectified.","Tissue — No infarction: the blockage wasn't complete or the clot dissolved. — Death of myocardial tissue, which is irreversible."]},{"s":"Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","a":"ischemia-infarction-atp","k":["Ischemia, Infarction & ATP▾","Ischemia","When the oxygen supply does not meet the heart's metabolic demands.","Infarction","Tissue death that results from that ischemia.","Why ATP Matters","ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a molecule that stores and transfers energy in cells; it is the main energy source for cellular function.","Myocardial cells cannot store ATP. They must be continuously fed ATP, so they need a continuous supply of oxygen to make it.","When oxygen goes down, ATP supply goes down, but the heart can't stop beating while ATP isn't being produced.","What Happens to Heart Tissue Over Time","Acute ischemia — ATP depletes instantly.","1 to 2 minutes — Decreased ability of the heart to contract.","30 minutes up to 4 hours — Irreversible injury to cardiac tissue. By 4 hours, necrosis has begun and the tissue begins to die.","1 to 2 weeks — The necrotic tissue is cleared away; the myocardium is very weak and susceptible to rupture.","About 6 weeks — The necrotic tissue has been replaced by tough scar tissue.","Reperfusion: as quickly as possible, at least within 4 hours. Most guidelines state reperfusion therapy should be done within four hours. Within 30 minutes would be ideal, since irreversible injury can occur that quickly.","Scar tissue is thickened and tougher, so electrical impulses have a harder time traveling through it. That is why ECGs after an MI can show permanent changes."]},{"s":"Zones of Damage▾","a":"zones-of-damage","k":["Zones of Damage▾","Infarction — Necrosis; dead cells, where the MI happened. — No. The goal is to stop it from increasing.","Injury — Not dead yet, but the tissue has suffered injury. — Some recovery is still possible; it can be perfused and restored to be viable.","Ischemia — The outer zone. — Full recovery is possible if it is perfused again.","The goal is to not extend the size of the infarct: increase oxygen and decrease the demand on the heart. The instructor compares this with an ischemic stroke: the same concept of tissue dying from lack of oxygen, where time is of the essenc…","Three Factors That Decide the Extent of Damage","The location or level of the occlusion in the coronary artery.","The length of time it has been occluded.","The availability of collateral circulation. In people with atherosclerotic plaque the heart can start to develop collateral circulation. The lecture calls it \"typically a bad thing,\" but here it creates ways to bypass the blockage and still…"]},{"s":"STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","a":"stemi-vs-nstemi","k":["STEMI vs NSTEMI▾","The main difference is the ST segment on the ECG.","ST segment — Elevated. — Normal, or a slight ST depression.","QRS complex — Usually a wide QRS that develops over hours. — Normal.","T wave — Peaked, then over time less peaked, then inverted. — Inverted the entire time.","Troponin — Elevated; still elevated after 2 hours, with a sharper increase than in NSTEMI. — Elevated.","Infarct size — Typically larger; more blockage, more tissue dying. — Smaller.","Outcomes — Worse. — Better than STEMI.","The \"tombstone.\" Nurses describe the ST elevation of a STEMI as looking like a tombstone, because the prognosis is not good if it isn't treated very quickly. Seeing it on an ECG should throw up red flags: investigate, and do it quickly.","On the ECG images, an NSTEMI can show ST depression or T wave inversion; a STEMI shows ST elevation.","Pericarditis can also show ST elevation and can look like an MI. See Myocarditis, Pericarditis & Cardiac Tamponade"]},{"s":"The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","a":"the-lad-the-widow-maker","k":["The LAD: The \"Widow-Maker\"▾","Coronary blood supply named in the lecture","The right coronary artery.","The left main artery, which branches into the left circumflex artery and the left anterior descending (LAD) artery.","The LAD supplies almost the entire front side of the heart, much of the sidewall, and most of the left ventricle.","The LAD is the vessel most commonly involved in MI, and one of the most damaging. It is a really large vessel feeding most of the left ventricle, so a blockage there is particularly detrimental and often very lethal if not treated very quic…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-acute-mi-pharm.html","t":"Acute MI Pharmacology","secs":[{"s":"Acute MI Pharmacology","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Initial Drug Treatment","Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)","Nitroglycerin Safety","Reperfusion Injury","Key Takeaways","Self-Test Flashcards","The second, shorter part of the ACS voiceover. It covers the initial drug treatment of an acute MI (oxygen, morphine, aspirin, nitroglycerin, beta blockers and thrombolytics), fibrinolytic therapy with alteplase, reperfusion injury, and the…","The pathophysiology of ACS and MI is covered in Acute Coronary Syndrome & Myocardial Infarction"]},{"s":"Initial Drug Treatment▾","a":"initial-drug-treatment","k":["Initial Drug Treatment▾","The lecture goes through these drugs twice, once briefly and once as a recap with more detail. Both passes are combined here.","Oxygen — Increases oxygen delivery to the ischemic myocardium. — New research shows that if the patient is not hypoxic, oxygen may not have that benefit. Whether it is ordered depends on the provider.","Morphine — Decreases pain. Some research suggests it dilates blood vessels to help blood flow, and there is some thought that it helps preserve ischemic tissue. — Side effects: respiratory depression and hypotension, so check vital signs an…","Aspirin — Makes platelets less sticky and suppresses platelet aggregation, so the thrombus is less likely to form or get bigger. — Routinely used. Have the patient chew the first dose to speed absorption. Research shows aspirin decreases mo…","Nitroglycerin — Dilates the veins to decrease preload; reduces preload and afterload, which helps limit infarct size. — It does not reduce mortality. Given sublingually, or IV if the patient can maintain their blood pressure.","Beta blockers — Reduce heart rate and contractility (and blood pressure), which reduces overall oxygen demand. This can reduce pain, reduce infarct size and help with mortality. — Only if there are no contraindications.","Thrombolytic agent — The biggest line of treatment. — Only if the patient is eligible, with no contraindications; for example, not after a recent hemorrhagic stroke. Ideally given within about 4 to 6 hours of the chest pain starting. These …","Take vital signs before giving any of these. Note heart rate, blood pressure and respiratory effort before administration. These medications can push someone over the edge if they aren't stable to begin with.","Which Drugs Affect Mortality","Aspirin — Decreases mortality in STEMI.","Beta blockers — Can help with mortality.","Nitroglycerin — Does not reduce mortality (it limits infarct size)."]},{"s":"Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","a":"fibrinolytic-therapy-alteplase-tpa","k":["Fibrinolytic Therapy: Alteplase (tPA)▾","When it's used","Often used in a STEMI.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is the first choice for treating a STEMI. If a cath lab is not available, fibrinolytic therapy is effective.","These are the \"clot busters.\"","Alteplase (tPA)","The prototype and the drug of choice; it is the most effective.","Dissolves the clot by converting plasminogen into plasmin; plasmin is what actually dissolves the clot.","Works best when given within 30 to 70 minutes of the onset of symptoms (when the chest pain started).","Drugs Given Alongside","In a STEMI where fibrinolytic therapy is anticipated, clopidogrel is also given at presentation, and heparin might be started.","Anyone who has had fibrinolytic therapy is given heparin and antiplatelet therapy (like clopidogrel) to help prevent new clots from forming. Alteplase dissolves the current clot; the others keep new ones from forming.","The main adverse effect is bleeding. We are dissolving a clot, so watch for bleeding.","The lecture gives two time windows: thrombolytics \"ideally\" within about 4 to 6 hours of chest pain starting, and alteplase working best within 30 to 70 minutes of symptom onset. Both are recorded here exactly as stated."]},{"s":"Nitroglycerin Safety▾","a":"nitroglycerin-safety","k":["Nitroglycerin Safety▾","Sildenafil (Viagra) — Do not give nitroglycerin with it. Both cause vasodilation of the veins and can reduce preload, leading to really profound hypotension.","Other nitrates — Severe hypotension, especially when taken with other nitrates.","Common effects — Headache and flushing.","Route — Sublingual, or IV if the blood pressure tolerates it."]},{"s":"Reperfusion Injury▾","a":"reperfusion-injury","k":["Reperfusion Injury▾","What the lecture says","When myocardial tissue that has been deprived of oxygen suddenly receives oxygenated blood, it can become stunned, and that can cause heart failure.","Why: white blood cells come to the infarcted, damaged tissue (that's their job), and they release oxidized free radicals that cause the reperfusion injury.","These patients need to be monitored closely.","Also monitor for reperfusion dysrhythmias, such as ventricular tachycardia (VTach) and ventricular fibrillation."]},{"s":"Key Takeaways▾","a":"key-takeaways","k":["Key Takeaways▾","Acute MI by the numbers","Acute MI is a leading cause of death in the United States and throughout the world.","A big reason for that mortality is hesitating or delaying care (\"it might pass,\" \"it's not serious\").","With treatment, survival is great: about 90 to 95% for treated patients in the US. Teach patients: if you suspect an MI, seek treatment. Better safe than sorry.","About half of people with MI are younger than 65.","People older than 65 and people with diabetes can have an MI and not know it: a silent or asymptomatic MI, found later, with no signs or symptoms at the time."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-aneurysms.html","t":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","secs":[{"s":"Aneurysms & Aortic Dissection","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","What an Aneurysm Is","Types of Aneurysms","Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)","Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection","Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection","Managing Aortic Dissection","Self-Test Flashcards","This page follows the aneurysm voiceover: what an aneurysm is and why it forms, risk factors, the types by location and by shape (saccular, berry, fusiform and false aneurysms), assessing an abdominal aortic aneurysm, and the two big compli…"]},{"s":"What an Aneurysm Is▾","a":"what-an-aneurysm-is","k":["What an Aneurysm Is▾","Definition and cause","A localized dilation or outpouching of a vessel wall.","A true aneurysm involves all three layers of the arterial wall. There are also false aneurysms (below).","Aneurysms are primarily a result of hypertension. Prolonged, uncontrolled high blood pressure puts constant pressure on the arterial wall: the vessel dilates, the wall weakens, a pouch or bulge forms, and eventually the pouch can burst.","Aneurysms cause turbulent blood flow, which makes them susceptible to rupture.","Most commonly seen in the aorta and the cerebral arteries, but they can occur elsewhere.","Listen for a bruit. Because of the turbulent flow, a stethoscope over an aneurysm may pick up a bruit: a whooshing-type sound.","Risk Factors","Think of anything that damages blood vessels. The primary drivers are atherosclerosis and hypertension.","Named in the lecture","Atherosclerosis","Hypertension","Diseases of the blood vessels","Trauma","Tobacco use","Older adult age (over 65)","A hereditary component"]},{"s":"Types of Aneurysms▾","a":"types-of-aneurysms","k":["Types of Aneurysms▾","By location","Aortic aneurysms, the most common type: abdominal aortic aneurysm (AAA, \"triple A\"), thoracic aortic aneurysm, and dissecting aortic aneurysm.","Cerebral aneurysms: anywhere in the brain.","False or pseudoaneurysms.","By shape","Saccular","Berry (a subtype of saccular)","Fusiform","Saccular — Balloon-shaped. Involves only one side of the vessel wall, not the entire circumference. A wide neck at the base. — Seen on an angiogram (dye is injected and watched). — Not specified separately in the lecture.","Berry — A subtype of saccular: the same pouch or balloon look, but a small neck at the base. — Located at bifurcations. A very common location is the circle of Willis, which connects the anterior and posterior circulation at the base of the…","Fusiform — Involves the entire circumference of the vessel; no longer limited to one wall. Usually a gradual, progressive dilation of the arterial wall. Can have potentially extensive involvement. — Often related to diffuse sclerotic change…","False (pseudo) — A localized dissection or tear in the inner artery wall; the outer artery walls are still intact. Considered a type of hematoma. — Often a result of vascular interventional procedures, such as leakage between a vascular gra…","Berry Aneurysm Surgery","Drain — Takes the blood out of the aneurysm.","Clip — Places a clip around the base of the aneurysm.","Coil — Fills the aneurysm from the inside with a coil."]},{"s":"Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","a":"abdominal-aortic-aneurysm-aaa","k":["Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA)▾","Auscultation — Listen with the stethoscope over the abdominal aorta. A bruit there suggests a AAA may be present.","Inspection — In particularly thin patients with low body fat, you may even see a pulsatile mass in the abdomen.","Light palpation — You could feel a mass that might indicate a AAA.","Suspected AAA: no deep palpation. Deep palpation is a risk for rupture, so keep it to light palpation.","The aorta is the largest vessel in the body and supplies a lot of really important places, so the clinical manifestations of an aortic aneurysm depend on where it is located. An aneurysm above the kidneys looks different from one below the …"]},{"s":"Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","a":"complications-rupture-aortic-dissection","k":["Complications: Rupture & Aortic Dissection▾","Rupture","Especially in the aorta, where the pressure in the vessel is very high.","If a AAA ruptures, the patient will bleed out very, very quickly, with a lot of blood involved.","Aortic dissection (dissecting aneurysm)","A tear in the inner layer of the artery; blood enters between the layers of the artery wall.","The turbulent blood flow tears away at the layers, separating them.","A medical emergency. These patients can also rupture, and without quick intervention it can be fatal.","An aneurysm increases the risk for dissection: a weakened wall can keep weakening, eventually tear, and turn into a dissection.","Not tested: the slides showing the different types of dissections and aneurysms. The instructor said, \"you do not need to know these. So don't memorize them. They're just for you to look at.\""]},{"s":"Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","a":"aneurysm-vs-aortic-dissection","k":["Aneurysm vs Aortic Dissection▾","Aneurysm","Can be completely asymptomatic.","Aortic dissection","Sudden, severe tearing pain: the most common manifestation.","Most likely felt in the chest, but it can also be in the neck, jaw, back or abdomen.","Clinical Manifestations of Aortic Dissection","Early stages — Blood pressure is elevated, as the body tries to compensate for the loss of blood.","Later stages — Blood pressure is \"in the trash\" and may be completely unattainable, because of significant bleeding. Blood between the walls of the artery isn't in the arteries getting where it needs to be.","Other signs — Syncope, total unresponsiveness, hemiplegia, paralysis of the lower extremities.","Without intervention — Progresses to total cardiovascular collapse, shock and death."]},{"s":"Managing Aortic Dissection▾","a":"managing-aortic-dissection","k":["Managing Aortic Dissection▾","Aortic dissection is a surgical emergency; minutes matter. Emergent evaluation is needed, and surgical intervention is the priority and the most important goal.","Diagnosis — CT scan, or MRA (an MRI with angiogram).","Beta blockers — Decrease heart rate and blood pressure to prevent further tearing or further dissection of the artery.","Nitrates — May also be seen.","Surgery — The most important goal of treatment.","Beta blockers and nitroglycerin in acute MI are a different use, covered in Acute MI Pharmacology"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]}];
